--  The network: internet addresses and their text, and a concept beside
--  core/io's and outside `world` through which a program connects,
--  listens, accepts and sends and receives over TCP and UDP.  A provider
--  without a network, and a firmware image that never asks for one,
--  carries none of it.  The provider resolves a name to its addresses;
--  names.ldn reads a hosts file and a resolver's settings and asks name
--  servers over any network, as a provider without the C library does.

import core/cmp
import core/io
import core/mem
import core/text
import core/time

--- The text of an address could not be read as one.  It is neither four
--- decimal parts from 0 to 255 nor an IPv6 address's groups; its zone is
--- empty, longer than `zone_room`, holds a NUL or follows an IPv4 address;
--- or its port is missing, above 65535 or not decimal.
public invalid_address: atom
--- Nothing listens at the address a connection was asked for: the system
--- answered it at once, as a closed port is answered.
public refused: atom
--- No route leads to the address: its network or its host cannot be
--- reached from here.
public unreachable: atom
--- Another socket already holds the address and port: a listener or a
--- datagram socket bound there.
public address_in_use: atom
--- The address cannot be used here: no interface of this system holds it,
--- its zone names no interface of this system, or the system does not
--- have its family at all, as one without IPv6 does not.
public address_unavailable: atom
--- The time limit `set_timeouts` gave passed before anything arrived or
--- could be sent, the limit `connect_within` was given passed before the
--- connection was made, or a connection's handshake took longer than the
--- system waits.  A socket `set_timeouts` limited is still usable.
public timed_out: atom
--- The connection's peer has gone: it closed or reset the connection, so
--- what is sent cannot be delivered.  A write to such a connection is this
--- atom on a hosted system, never the end of the process.
public disconnected: atom
--- No address answers to the name: neither the hosts file nor a name
--- server knows it, or it cannot be a host's name at all, being empty,
--- longer than 253 bytes or holding an empty label or one longer than 63.
public unknown_name: atom
--- The system does not have what was asked: an option it does not set, as
--- Windows sets no `reuse_port`, or a Unix-domain socket of a kind it does
--- not make, as Windows makes no datagram one.
public unsupported: atom

--- An IPv4 address: four bytes, `127.0.0.1` in its text.
public ipv4: atom
--- An IPv6 address: sixteen bytes, `::1` in its text.
public ipv6: atom
--- What an address is: `ipv4` or `ipv6`.
public address_family: type = ipv4 | ipv6

--- The most bytes a zone has: an interface's name on every hosted
--- system, whose own limit, IFNAMSIZ, counts a NUL after them.
public zone_room: usize = 15

--- An internet address and a port: TCP's and UDP's endpoint.  `family`
--- says which; an IPv4 address is `octets`' first four bytes, in the
--- order its text writes them, and the other twelve are zero; an IPv6
--- address is all sixteen, in network order.  `port` is a number from 0
--- to 65535, and 0 asks the system for a free one when a socket is bound.
--- `zone` is an IPv6 address's zone, the interface a link-local address
--- such as `fe80::1%eth0` is reached through: the name's bytes, at most
--- `zone_room` of them, and zeros after it, or all zeros for none, which
--- every IPv4 address has.  A zone of decimal digits is the interface's
--- number, `fe80::1%2`, and a provider turns a name into the number its
--- system gives the interface when the address is used (D581).  Make one
--- with `ipv4_address`, `ipv6_address`, `loopback`, `unspecified`,
--- `parse_ip` or `parse_address`, give it a zone with `with_zone`, and
--- compare two with `same_address`.  An address is `cmp.equatable` and
--- `cmp.hashable` as `same_address` compares, zone included, so a map
--- keys by it.
public address: type = struct
    family: address_family
    octets: [16]u8
    port: u16
    zone: [16]u8
end address

--- What arrived in one `receive_from`: how many bytes, and the address and
--- port of the socket that sent them.
public datagram: type = struct
    count: usize
    sender: address
end datagram

--- Stop receiving: what arrives later is thrown away.
public receiving: atom
--- Stop sending: the peer reads the end of the stream once what was sent
--- before has arrived, while this side may still receive.
public sending: atom
--- Stop both receiving and sending.
public both_directions: atom
--- Which way of a connection `shutdown` stops: `receiving`, `sending` or
--- `both_directions`.
public direction: type = receiving | sending | both_directions

--- Let a socket bind an address and port that earlier connections still
--- hold while they close, SO_REUSEADDR.  Set it before the socket is
--- bound; every listener of a hosted provider has it already.
public reuse_address: atom
--- Let several sockets bind one address and port, SO_REUSEPORT, the
--- system sharing what arrives among them.  Each sets it before it is
--- bound.  Windows has none, and it is `unsupported` there.
public reuse_port: atom
--- Probe an idle connection now and then, and end it once its peer stops
--- answering, SO_KEEPALIVE.  `keep_alive_idle` and `keep_alive_interval`
--- say how often.
public keep_alive: atom
--- Let a datagram socket send to a broadcast address, SO_BROADCAST.
public broadcast: atom
--- Let an IPv6 socket take IPv6 alone, never IPv4 through mapped
--- addresses, IPV6_V6ONLY.  Every IPv6 socket of a hosted provider is
--- made with it on.  An IPv4 socket fails `io_failed` for it.
public ipv6_only: atom
--- A socket option that is on or off, `set_flag`'s and `flag`'s.
public flag_option: type = reuse_address | reuse_port | keep_alive | broadcast
    | ipv6_only

--- How many bytes the system keeps for what arrives, SO_RCVBUF.  The
--- system rounds what it is given, and Linux doubles it, so read it back.
public receive_buffer: atom
--- How many bytes the system keeps for what is sent, SO_SNDBUF, rounded as
--- `receive_buffer` is.
public send_buffer: atom
--- How many routers what a socket sends to one address may cross, IP_TTL
--- or IPV6_UNICAST_HOPS by the socket's family: 1 to 255.
public unicast_hops: atom
--- How many routers a datagram sent to a multicast group may cross,
--- IP_MULTICAST_TTL or IPV6_MULTICAST_HOPS: 0 to 255, and 1, this
--- network alone, until it is set.
public multicast_hops: atom
--- A socket option that is a number, `set_number`'s and `number`'s.
public number_option: type = receive_buffer | send_buffer | unicast_hops
    | multicast_hops

--- How long a connection with `keep_alive` is idle before its first
--- probe, TCP_KEEPIDLE, or TCP_KEEPALIVE on Darwin.
public keep_alive_idle: atom
--- How long a connection with `keep_alive` waits between probes,
--- TCP_KEEPINTVL.
public keep_alive_interval: atom
--- A socket option that is a span of time, kept in whole seconds:
--- `set_span`'s and `span`'s.
public span_option: type = keep_alive_idle | keep_alive_interval

--- The network: TCP connections and UDP datagram sockets over IPv4 and
--- IPv6, each a `file` of the provider.  It is a reader and a writer of
--- them as well, so a connection is read and written through `io.read`
--- and `io.write`, a line reader and a buffered writer among them, whose
--- failures are `io_failed` with the provider's detail; `send` and
--- `receive` say more, `timed_out` and `disconnected`.  A connection's
--- `read` and `receive` return zero once its peer has finished sending.
---
--- Every call waits until it can do what it was asked, as a blocking
--- socket does: `accept` for a connection, `receive` for bytes, `send` for
--- room, unless `set_timeouts` gave the socket a limit.  A listener takes
--- connections whose handshake the system completes on its own, so one
--- thread can connect to its own listener and then accept.  A connection's
--- sends wait only once the system's buffers are full.  Close every socket
--- with `close`; one dropped unclosed is open until the process
--- ends, since there are no destructors.
public network: type = concept (provider: type) is io.reader, io.writer
    --- Connect to a listener at `to` and return the connection.  A port
    --- nothing listens on is `refused`.
    connect: (self: provider, to: address) -> (connected: io.file)
             ! refused | unreachable | address_unavailable | timed_out
             | io.io_failed
    --- Connect as `connect` does, but fail `timed_out` once `limit` has
    --- passed without the connection being made; a limit of zero is no
    --- limit, as `set_timeouts` reads one (D580).
    connect_within: (self: provider, to: address, limit: time.duration)
                    -> (connected: io.file)
                    ! refused | unreachable | address_unavailable | timed_out
                    | io.io_failed
    --- Listen for connections at `at`; port 0 asks for a free one, which
    --- `local_address` then gives.  A listener may take an address its
    --- earlier connections still hold while they close, but never one
    --- another socket listens at, which is `address_in_use`.
    listen: (self: provider, at: address) -> (listener: io.file)
            ! address_in_use | address_unavailable | io.no_access | io.io_failed
    --- Take the next connection made to a listener, waiting for one.
    accept: (self: provider, listener: io.file) -> (accepted: io.file)
            ! timed_out | io.io_failed
    --- Open a UDP socket bound to `at`, port 0 for a free one.
    open_datagram: (self: provider, at: address) -> (socket: io.file)
                   ! address_in_use | address_unavailable | io.no_access
                   | io.io_failed
    --- Send all of `bytes` over a connection, waiting while its buffers
    --- are full.  A failure may follow a part of them.
    send: (self: provider, connection: io.file, bytes: []u8) -> none
          ! disconnected | timed_out | io.io_failed
    --- Receive what arrives next on a connection into `into`, waiting for
    --- something: its count, zero once the peer has finished sending.
    receive: (self: provider, connection: io.file, into: []mut u8)
             -> (count: usize) ! disconnected | timed_out | io.io_failed
    --- Send `bytes` as one datagram to `to`.  A datagram longer than the
    --- system takes cannot be sent whole and is `io_failed`.
    send_to: (self: provider, socket: io.file, bytes: []u8, to: address)
             -> none ! unreachable | timed_out | io.io_failed
    --- Receive the next datagram into `into`, waiting for one, and say
    --- who sent it.  A datagram longer than `into` is cut to it, the rest
    --- thrown away.
    receive_from: (self: provider, socket: io.file, into: []mut u8)
                  -> (received: datagram) ! timed_out | io.io_failed
    --- Stop receiving, sending or both on a connection.
    shutdown: (self: provider, connection: io.file, stopping: direction)
              -> none ! io.io_failed
    --- The address and port a socket is bound to.
    local_address: (self: provider, socket: io.file) -> (at: address)
                   ! io.io_failed
    --- The address and port of a connection's peer.
    peer_address: (self: provider, connection: io.file) -> (at: address)
                  ! io.io_failed
    --- Limit how long `socket` waits: `receive_limit` for a receive or an
    --- accept, `send_limit` for a send; a span of zero is no limit.
    set_timeouts: (self: provider, socket: io.file, receive_limit: time.duration,
                   send_limit: time.duration) -> none ! io.io_failed
    --- With `on`, send a connection's small writes at once rather than
    --- gather them while an earlier one is unacknowledged.
    set_no_delay: (self: provider, connection: io.file, on: bool) -> none
                  ! io.io_failed
    --- Close a socket, which is consumed even when this fails.
    close: (self: provider, sink socket: io.file) -> none ! io.io_failed
    --- Turn the option `which` on or off for `socket`.
    set_flag: (self: provider, socket: io.file, which: flag_option, on: bool)
              -> none ! unsupported | io.io_failed
    --- Whether the option `which` is on for `socket`.
    flag: (self: provider, socket: io.file, which: flag_option)
          -> (on: bool) ! unsupported | io.io_failed
    --- Set the option `which` of `socket` to `value`.
    set_number: (self: provider, socket: io.file, which: number_option,
                 value: u32) -> none ! unsupported | io.io_failed
    --- The option `which` of `socket`, as the system keeps it.
    number: (self: provider, socket: io.file, which: number_option)
            -> (value: u32) ! unsupported | io.io_failed
    --- Set the option `which` of `socket` to `value`, kept in whole seconds,
    --- a part of one rounded up; zero is `io_failed`.
    set_span: (self: provider, socket: io.file, which: span_option,
               value: time.duration) -> none ! unsupported | io.io_failed
    --- The option `which` of `socket`, in whole seconds.
    span: (self: provider, socket: io.file, which: span_option)
          -> (value: time.duration) ! unsupported | io.io_failed
    --- With `on`, make closing `socket` wait up to `limit`, kept in whole
    --- seconds, for what it has sent to be delivered, SO_LINGER, and a
    --- limit of zero reset the connection at the close.  Without, a close
    --- returns at once and the system delivers what is left on its own.
    set_linger: (self: provider, socket: io.file, on: bool,
                 limit: time.duration) -> none ! io.io_failed
    --- Whether closing `socket` lingers, and how long.
    linger: (self: provider, socket: io.file)
            -> (on: bool, limit: time.duration) ! io.io_failed
    --- Make the datagram socket `socket` take what is sent to the multicast
    --- group `group`, through the interface whose address `through` is for
    --- IPv4, or whose name or number `through`'s zone is for IPv6; an
    --- unspecified `through` without a zone lets the system choose.  An
    --- address that is no group is `address_unavailable`.
    join_group: (self: provider, socket: io.file, group: address,
                 through: address) -> none ! address_unavailable | io.io_failed
    --- Stop taking what is sent to a group `join_group` joined.
    leave_group: (self: provider, socket: io.file, group: address,
                  through: address) -> none ! address_unavailable | io.io_failed
    set_multicast_interface: (self: provider, socket: io.file, through: address) -> none ! address_unavailable | io.io_failed
    multicast_interface: (self: provider, socket: io.file) -> (through: address) ! io.io_failed
    --- Write into `into` the addresses the host name `name` has, each with
    --- `port`, in the order the system's resolver gives them, and return
    --- how many, as many as `into` holds.  It waits as a receive does while
    --- name servers are asked, which may take seconds, and fails
    --- `timed_out` when none answers.
    resolve: (self: provider, name: []u8, port: u16, into: []mut address)
             -> (count: usize) ! unknown_name | timed_out | io.io_failed
    --- Connect to the Unix-domain listener at `path`.  A path no socket
    --- listens at is `refused`; an empty one, one holding a NUL or one
    --- longer than the system takes is `invalid_address`.
    connect_local: (self: provider, path: text.os_string)
                   -> (connected: io.file)
                   ! refused | invalid_address | unsupported | io.no_access
                   | io.io_failed
    --- Listen for Unix-domain connections at `path`, which the listener
    --- makes and which stays after it closes, for the program to remove.  A
    --- path that already exists is `address_in_use`.
    listen_local: (self: provider, path: text.os_string)
                  -> (listener: io.file)
                  ! address_in_use | invalid_address | unsupported
                  | io.no_access | io.io_failed
    --- Open a Unix-domain datagram socket bound to `path`, as
    --- `listen_local` binds one.
    open_local_datagram: (self: provider, path: text.os_string)
                         -> (socket: io.file)
                         ! address_in_use | invalid_address | unsupported
                         | io.no_access | io.io_failed
    --- Send `bytes` as one datagram to the Unix-domain socket at `path`.  A
    --- path no socket is bound to is `refused`.
    send_to_local: (self: provider, socket: io.file, bytes: []u8,
                    path: text.os_string)
                   -> none ! refused | invalid_address | timed_out | io.io_failed
    --- Receive the next datagram of a Unix-domain datagram socket into
    --- `into`, waiting for one, and the path of the socket that sent it
    --- into `room`, lent from it: empty when the sender is bound to none.
    receive_from_local: (self: provider, socket: io.file, into: []mut u8,
                         room: []mut u8)
                        -> (count: usize, sender: text.os_string from room)
                        ! timed_out | io.io_failed
end network

--- An IPv4 address `a.b.c.d` and a port.
public ipv4_address: (a: u8, b: u8, c: u8, d: u8, port: u16)
                     -> (at: address) =
    at = (family: ipv4, octets: zeroed, port: port, zone: zeroed)
    at.octets[0] = a
    at.octets[1] = b
    at.octets[2] = c
    at.octets[3] = d
end ipv4_address

--- An IPv6 address of eight 16-bit groups, in the order its text writes
--- them, and a port, with no zone.
public ipv6_address: (groups: [8]u16, port: u16) -> (at: address) =
    at = (family: ipv6, octets: zeroed, port: port, zone: zeroed)
    for index in usize(0)..<8 do
        at.octets[2 * index] = u8(groups[index] >> 8)
        at.octets[2 * index + 1] = u8(groups[index] & 255)
    end for
end ipv6_address

--- The loopback address of `family`, `127.0.0.1` or `::1`, and a port:
--- this system, reached without a network.
public loopback: (family: address_family, port: u16) -> (at: address) =
    at = (family: family, octets: zeroed, port: port, zone: zeroed)
    if family == ipv4 then
        at.octets[0] = 127
        at.octets[3] = 1
    else
        at.octets[15] = 1
    end if
end loopback

--- The unspecified address of `family`, `0.0.0.0` or `::`, and a port: a
--- socket bound to it takes what arrives at the port on every interface.
public unspecified: (family: address_family, port: u16) -> (at: address) =
    at = (family: family, octets: zeroed, port: port, zone: zeroed)
end unspecified

--- The address `at` with another port.
public with_port: (at: address, port: u16) -> (result: address) =
    result = at
    result.port = port
end with_port

--- The address `at` with the zone `name`, an interface's name or its
--- number in decimal.  It fails `invalid_address` for an IPv4 address, and
--- for a name `parse_ip` would not read after a `%`.  An empty name takes
--- the zone away.
public with_zone: (at: address, name: []u8) -> (result: address)
                  ! invalid_address =
    result = at
    result.zone = zeroed
    return when lenof name == 0
    fail invalid_address when at.family <> ipv6
    try zone_into(result, name)
end with_zone

--- How many bytes the address's zone has, zero for none: the zone is
--- `at.zone[0..<net.zone_length(at)]`.
public zone_length: (at: address) -> (length: usize) =
    length = mem.find(at.zone[0..<16], 0)
end zone_length

--- Whether two addresses are the same family, address, zone and port.
public same_address: (left: address, right: address) -> (same: bool) =
    same = left.family == right.family and left.port == right.port
    for index in usize(0)..<16 do
        if left.octets[index] <> right.octets[index]
          or left.zone[index] <> right.zone[index] then
            same = false
        end if
    end for
end same_address

--  An address is equal as `same_address` says, and hashes the bytes that
--  equality reads with `cmp.hash_bytes`, as `[]u8` does: the family, the
--  sixteen octets, the port high byte first and the zone (D1031).
hash_address: (value: address, seed: cmp.hash_seed) -> (result: u64) =
    mut read: [35]u8 = zeroed
    read[0] = if value.family == ipv4 then 4 else 6 end if
    for index in usize(0)..<16 do
        read[1 + index] = value.octets[index]
        read[19 + index] = value.zone[index]
    end for
    read[17] = u8(value.port >> 8)
    read[18] = u8(value.port & 255)
    result = cmp.hash_bytes(read[0..<35], seed)
end hash_address

address is cmp.equatable (equal: same_address)
address is cmp.hashable (hash: hash_address)

--- Whether an address is its family's unspecified address, whatever its
--- port.
public is_unspecified: (at: address) -> (yes: bool) =
    yes = true
    for index in usize(0)..<16 do
        if at.octets[index] <> 0 then yes = false end if
    end for
end is_unspecified

--  An IPv4 address's four parts: decimal, from 0 to 255, with no leading
--  zero, which some readers take for octal.  Each part is core/text's to
--  parse.
ipv4_octets: (source: []u8) -> (octets: [4]u8) ! invalid_address =
    octets = zeroed
    mut part: usize = 0
    mut start: usize = 0
    for at in usize(0)..<lenof source + 1 do
        if at == lenof source or source[at] == u8('.') then
            fail invalid_address when part == 4
            piece: []u8 = source[start..<at]
            fail invalid_address when lenof piece == 0
              or (lenof piece > 1 and piece[0] == u8('0'))
            octets[part] = text.parse_u8(piece) else (problem)
                _ = problem
                fail invalid_address
            end
            inc part
            start = at + 1
        end if
    end for
    fail invalid_address when part <> 4
end ipv4_octets

--  Where in SOURCE, from AT, the next `:` is, or its length.
next_colon: (source: []u8, at: usize) -> (found: usize) =
    found = at
    while found < lenof source and source[found] <> u8(':') do
        inc found
    end while
end next_colon

--  An IPv6 address's sixteen bytes, from its groups: one to four
--  hexadecimal digits each, at most one `::` standing for one or more
--  zero groups, and an IPv4 address in the last 32 bits.
ipv6_octets: (source: []u8) -> (octets: [16]u8) ! invalid_address =
    octets = zeroed
    mut groups: [8]u16 = zeroed
    mut count: usize = 0
    mut gap: usize = 9
    mut at: usize = 0
    length: usize = lenof source
    fail invalid_address when length < 2
    if source[0] == u8(':') then
        fail invalid_address when source[1] <> u8(':')
        gap = 0
        at = 2
    end if
    while at < length do
        fail invalid_address when count == 8
        ends: usize = next_colon(source, at)
        piece: []u8 = source[at..<ends]
        if ends == length and lenof piece > 4 then
            --  An IPv4 address is the last 32 bits, and nothing follows it.
            fail invalid_address when count > 6
            four: [4]u8 = try ipv4_octets(piece)
            groups[count] = (u16(four[0]) << 8) | u16(four[1])
            groups[count + 1] = (u16(four[2]) << 8) | u16(four[3])
            count += 2
            at = length
            break
        end if
        fail invalid_address when lenof piece == 0 or lenof piece > 4
        value: u32 = text.parse_hex_u32(piece) else (problem)
            _ = problem
            fail invalid_address
        end
        groups[count] = u16(value)
        inc count
        at = ends
        break when at == length
        --  A `:` follows the group: another group, or a second `:`.
        inc at
        fail invalid_address when at == length
        if source[at] == u8(':') then
            fail invalid_address when gap <> 9
            gap = count
            inc at
        end if
    end while
    if gap == 9 then
        fail invalid_address when count <> 8
    else
        fail invalid_address when count > 7
        --  The groups after the gap move to the end.
        moved: usize = count - gap
        mut index: usize = 0
        while index < moved do
            groups[7 - index] = groups[count - 1 - index]
            groups[count - 1 - index] = 0
            inc index
        end while
    end if
    for index in usize(0)..<8 do
        octets[2 * index] = u8(groups[index] >> 8)
        octets[2 * index + 1] = u8(groups[index] & 255)
    end for
end ipv6_octets

--  Give `at`, whose zone is all zeros, the zone `name`: one to
--  `zone_room` bytes, none of them NUL, which ends a zone.
zone_into: (inout at: address, name: []u8) -> none ! invalid_address =
    length: usize = lenof name
    fail invalid_address when length == 0 or length > zone_room
      or mem.find(name, 0) < length
    mem.copy(at.zone[0..<length], name) else (problem)
        _ = problem
        fail invalid_address
    end
end zone_into

--  Read an IPv6 address into `at`, and the zone after its `%` when it has
--  one.
zoned: (inout at: address, source: []u8) -> none ! invalid_address =
    percent: usize = mem.find(source, u8('%'))
    at.family = ipv6
    at.octets = try ipv6_octets(source[0..<percent])
    if percent < lenof source then
        try zone_into(at, source[percent + 1..<lenof source])
    end if
end zoned

--- Read an address without a port from its text, `127.0.0.1`,
--- `2001:db8::1` or `fe80::1%eth0`, and give it port 0.  An IPv4 address
--- is four decimal parts from 0 to 255 without a leading zero.  An IPv6
--- address is groups of one to four hexadecimal digits in either case,
--- with at most one `::` and an IPv4 address in the last 32 bits, and
--- after them a zone: `%` and one to `zone_room` bytes, none of them NUL,
--- kept as they are written.  Anything else, a zone after an IPv4 address
--- and surrounding spaces among it, is `invalid_address`.
public parse_ip: (source: []u8) -> (at: address) ! invalid_address =
    at = unspecified(ipv4, 0)
    if mem.find(source, u8(':')) < lenof source then
        try zoned(at, source)
        return
    end if
    four: [4]u8 = try ipv4_octets(source)
    for index in usize(0)..<4 do
        at.octets[index] = four[index]
    end for
end parse_ip

--- Read an address and a port from their text: `127.0.0.1:8080`, or an
--- IPv6 address in brackets, `[::1]:8080` and `[fe80::1%eth0]:8080`.  The
--- port is decimal, from 0 to 65535; an address is read as `parse_ip`
--- reads one, its zone inside the brackets as written rather than as a
--- URL escapes it, `%25eth0`.  Anything else is `invalid_address`.
public parse_address: (source: []u8) -> (at: address) ! invalid_address =
    length: usize = lenof source
    mut colon: usize = length
    mut index: usize = length
    while index > 0 do
        dec index
        if source[index] == u8(':') then
            colon = index
            break
        end if
    end while
    fail invalid_address when colon == length or colon + 1 == length
    port: u16 = text.parse_u16(source[colon + 1..<length]) else (problem)
        _ = problem
        fail invalid_address
    end
    host: []u8 = source[0..<colon]
    if lenof host > 0 and host[0] == u8('[') then
        fail invalid_address when lenof host < 2
          or host[lenof host - 1] <> u8(']')
        at = unspecified(ipv6, port)
        try zoned(at, host[1..<lenof host - 1])
        return
    end if
    four: [4]u8 = try ipv4_octets(host)
    at = ipv4_address(four[0], four[1], four[2], four[3], port)
end parse_address

--  The IPv6 address's group INDEX.
group: (at: address, index: usize) -> (value: u32) =
    value = (u32(at.octets[2 * index]) << 8) | u32(at.octets[2 * index + 1])
end group

--  Append the IPv4 address in the four bytes from FIRST.
dotted: (inout into: text.builder, at: address, first: usize)
        -> none ! mem.out_of_memory =
    for index in usize(0)..<4 do
        if index > 0 then try text.append_byte(into, u8('.')) end if
        try text.append_u32(into, u32(at.octets[first + index]))
    end for
end dotted

--  Append an IPv6 address as RFC 5952 writes it: lower-case groups
--  without leading zeros, the longest run of two or more zero groups, the
--  first of equals, written `::`, and an IPv4-mapped address's last 32
--  bits in dotted decimal.
grouped: (inout into: text.builder, at: address) -> none ! mem.out_of_memory =
    mut mapped: bool = group(at, 5) == 65535
    for index in usize(0)..<5 do
        if group(at, index) <> 0 then mapped = false end if
    end for
    last: usize = if mapped then 6 else 8 end if
    mut best_start: usize = 8
    mut best_length: usize = 1
    mut index: usize = 0
    while index < last do
        if group(at, index) == 0 then
            mut ends: usize = index
            while ends < last and group(at, ends) == 0 do
                inc ends
            end while
            if ends - index > best_length then
                best_start = index
                best_length = ends - index
            end if
            index = ends
        else
            inc index
        end if
    end while
    index = 0
    while index < last do
        if index == best_start then
            try text.append(into, "::")
            index += best_length
        else
            if index > 0 and index <> best_start + best_length then
                try text.append_byte(into, u8(':'))
            end if
            try text.append_hex_u32(into, group(at, index))
            inc index
        end if
    end while
    if mapped then
        if best_start + best_length <> 6 then
            try text.append_byte(into, u8(':'))
        end if
        try dotted(into, at, 12)
    end if
end grouped

--- Append an address's text without its port: `127.0.0.1`, or an IPv6
--- address as RFC 5952 writes it, `::1` and `2001:db8::1`, lower case with
--- the longest run of zero groups shortened and an IPv4-mapped address as
--- `::ffff:192.0.2.1`, and its zone after a `%`, `fe80::1%eth0`.
--- `parse_ip` reads it back.  It is written whole or not at all.
public append_ip: (inout into: text.builder, at: address)
                  -> none ! mem.out_of_memory =
    start: usize = text.length(into)
    address_written(into, at, false) else (problem)
        text.truncate(into, start)
        fail problem
    end
end append_ip

--- Append an address's text with its port: `127.0.0.1:8080`, or an IPv6
--- address and its zone in brackets, `[::1]:8080` and
--- `[fe80::1%eth0]:8080`.  `parse_address` reads it back.  It is written
--- whole or not at all.
public append_address: (inout into: text.builder, at: address)
                       -> none ! mem.out_of_memory =
    start: usize = text.length(into)
    address_written(into, at, true) else (problem)
        text.truncate(into, start)
        fail problem
    end
end append_address

address_written: (inout into: text.builder, at: address, with_port: bool)
                 -> none ! mem.out_of_memory =
    bracketed: bool = with_port and at.family == ipv6
    if bracketed then try text.append_byte(into, u8('[')) end if
    if at.family == ipv4 then
        try dotted(into, at, 0)
    else
        try grouped(into, at)
        zone: usize = mem.find(at.zone[0..<16], 0)
        if zone > 0 then
            try text.append_byte(into, u8('%'))
            try text.append(into, at.zone[0..<zone])
        end if
    end if
    if bracketed then try text.append_byte(into, u8(']')) end if
    if with_port then
        try text.append_byte(into, u8(':'))
        try text.append_u32(into, u32(at.port))
    end if
end address_written

address_text: (self: ptr address, inout into: text.builder)
              -> none ! mem.out_of_memory =
    try append_address(into, self.val)
end address_text

address is text.formattable (append: address_text)

--- Connect to a listener at `to` through `network` evidence, waiting as
--- long as the system does.  Close the connection with `close`.
public connect: (provider: type is network, host: provider, to: address)
                -> (connected: io.file)
                ! refused | unreachable | address_unavailable | timed_out
                | io.io_failed =
    connected = try provider.connect(host, to)
end connect

--- Connect to a listener at `to`, failing `timed_out` once `limit` has
--- passed without an answer, as from a peer that never answers; a limit of
--- zero is no limit.  When the limit ends the attempt, its socket is
--- closed.  A connection that is made is as `connect` makes it, with no
--- limit of its own on what it sends and receives.
public connect_within: (provider: type is network, host: provider,
                        to: address, limit: time.duration)
                       -> (connected: io.file)
                       ! refused | unreachable | address_unavailable
                       | timed_out | io.io_failed =
    connected = try provider.connect_within(host, to, limit)
end connect_within

--- Listen for connections at `at`, port 0 for a free one; close the
--- listener with `close`.
public listen: (provider: type is network, host: provider, at: address)
               -> (listener: io.file)
               ! address_in_use | address_unavailable | io.no_access
               | io.io_failed =
    listener = try provider.listen(host, at)
end listen

--- Take the next connection made to `listener`, waiting for one unless a
--- receive limit was set.
public accept: (provider: type is network, host: provider, listener: io.file)
               -> (accepted: io.file) ! timed_out | io.io_failed =
    accepted = try provider.accept(host, listener)
end accept

--- Open a UDP socket bound to `at`, port 0 for a free one.
public open_datagram: (provider: type is network, host: provider,
                       at: address) -> (socket: io.file)
                      ! address_in_use | address_unavailable | io.no_access
                      | io.io_failed =
    socket = try provider.open_datagram(host, at)
end open_datagram

--- Send all of `bytes` over a connection.  A connection whose peer has
--- gone is `disconnected`.
public send: (provider: type is network, host: provider, connection: io.file,
              bytes: []u8) -> none ! disconnected | timed_out | io.io_failed =
    try provider.send(host, connection, bytes)
end send

--- Receive what arrives next on a connection into `into`.  It returns the
--- count, zero once the peer has finished sending.
public receive: (provider: type is network, host: provider,
                 connection: io.file, into: []mut u8)
                -> (count: usize) ! disconnected | timed_out | io.io_failed =
    count = try provider.receive(host, connection, into)
end receive

--- Send `bytes` as one datagram to `to`.
public send_to: (provider: type is network, host: provider, socket: io.file,
                 bytes: []u8, to: address)
                -> none ! unreachable | timed_out | io.io_failed =
    try provider.send_to(host, socket, bytes, to)
end send_to

--- Receive the next datagram into `into` and say who sent it.
public receive_from: (provider: type is network, host: provider,
                      socket: io.file, into: []mut u8)
                     -> (received: datagram) ! timed_out | io.io_failed =
    received = try provider.receive_from(host, socket, into)
end receive_from

--- Stop receiving, sending or both on a connection: `net.sending` lets
--- the peer read the end of what was sent.
public shutdown: (provider: type is network, host: provider,
                  connection: io.file, stopping: direction)
                 -> none ! io.io_failed =
    try provider.shutdown(host, connection, stopping)
end shutdown

--- The address and port a socket is bound to: a listener's or a datagram
--- socket's port when it was bound to port 0.
public local_address: (provider: type is network, host: provider,
                       socket: io.file) -> (at: address) ! io.io_failed =
    at = try provider.local_address(host, socket)
end local_address

--- The address and port of a connection's peer.
public peer_address: (provider: type is network, host: provider,
                      connection: io.file) -> (at: address) ! io.io_failed =
    at = try provider.peer_address(host, connection)
end peer_address

--- Limit how long a receive or an accept, and a send, waits on `socket`;
--- zero is no limit.  A limit that passes is `timed_out`.
public set_timeouts: (provider: type is network, host: provider,
                      socket: io.file, receive_limit: time.duration,
                      send_limit: time.duration) -> none ! io.io_failed =
    try provider.set_timeouts(host, socket, receive_limit, send_limit)
end set_timeouts

--- Send a connection's small writes at once rather than gather them.
public set_no_delay: (provider: type is network, host: provider,
                      connection: io.file, on: bool) -> none ! io.io_failed =
    try provider.set_no_delay(host, connection, on)
end set_no_delay

--- Close a socket.  Treat it as consumed even if closing reports
--- `io_failed`.
public close: (provider: type is network, host: provider,
               sink socket: io.file) -> none ! io.io_failed =
    try provider.close(host, socket)
end close

--- Turn the option `which` on or off for `socket`.
public set_flag: (provider: type is network, host: provider, socket: io.file,
                  which: flag_option, on: bool)
                 -> none ! unsupported | io.io_failed =
    try provider.set_flag(host, socket, which, on)
end set_flag

--- Whether the option `which` is on for `socket`.
public flag: (provider: type is network, host: provider, socket: io.file,
              which: flag_option) -> (on: bool) ! unsupported | io.io_failed =
    on = try provider.flag(host, socket, which)
end flag

--- Set the option `which` of `socket` to `value`: a buffer's bytes or a
--- count of hops.
public set_number: (provider: type is network, host: provider,
                    socket: io.file, which: number_option, value: u32)
                   -> none ! unsupported | io.io_failed =
    try provider.set_number(host, socket, which, value)
end set_number

--- The option `which` of `socket` as the system keeps it, which for a
--- buffer may differ from what was set.
public number: (provider: type is network, host: provider, socket: io.file,
                which: number_option)
               -> (value: u32) ! unsupported | io.io_failed =
    value = try provider.number(host, socket, which)
end number

--- Set the keep-alive span `which` of `socket`, kept in whole seconds.
public set_span: (provider: type is network, host: provider, socket: io.file,
                  which: span_option, value: time.duration)
                 -> none ! unsupported | io.io_failed =
    try provider.set_span(host, socket, which, value)
end set_span

--- The keep-alive span `which` of `socket`.
public span: (provider: type is network, host: provider, socket: io.file,
              which: span_option)
             -> (value: time.duration) ! unsupported | io.io_failed =
    value = try provider.span(host, socket, which)
end span

--- Make closing `socket` linger up to `limit` for what it sent, with
--- `on`, or return at once, without.
public set_linger: (provider: type is network, host: provider,
                    socket: io.file, on: bool, limit: time.duration)
                   -> none ! io.io_failed =
    try provider.set_linger(host, socket, on, limit)
end set_linger

--- Whether closing `socket` lingers, and how long.
public linger: (provider: type is network, host: provider, socket: io.file)
               -> (on: bool, limit: time.duration) ! io.io_failed =
    (on: held, limit: kept) := try provider.linger(host, socket)
    on = held
    limit = kept
end linger

--- Make a datagram socket take what is sent to the multicast group
--- `group`, through the interface `through` names, the unspecified
--- address for the system's choice.
public join_group: (provider: type is network, host: provider,
                    socket: io.file, group: address, through: address)
                   -> none ! address_unavailable | io.io_failed =
    try provider.join_group(host, socket, group, through)
end join_group

--- Stop taking what is sent to a group `join_group` joined.
public leave_group: (provider: type is network, host: provider,
                     socket: io.file, group: address, through: address)
                    -> none ! address_unavailable | io.io_failed =
    try provider.leave_group(host, socket, group, through)
end leave_group

--- Write into `into` the addresses `name` has, each with `port`, and
--- return how many.  A name that is an address's own text, `127.0.0.1` or
--- `::1`, is that address, read as `parse_ip` reads one and asking no
--- one; any other is the provider's to resolve, as `network`'s `resolve`
--- says.  A name of no address is `unknown_name`, and `into` without room
--- holds none and returns zero.
public resolve: (provider: type is network, host: provider, name: []u8,
                 port: u16, into: []mut address)
                -> (count: usize) ! unknown_name | timed_out | io.io_failed =
    count = 0
    literal: address = parse_ip(name) else (problem)
        _ = problem
        return when lenof into == 0
        count = try provider.resolve(host, name, port, into)
        fail unknown_name when count == 0
        return
    end
    return when lenof into == 0
    into[0] = with_port(literal, port)
    count = 1
end resolve

--  Why the last of `connect_by_name`'s attempts failed.
attempt_failure: type = refused | unreachable | address_unavailable | timed_out
    | io.io_failed

--- Connect to the host `name` at `port`: resolve it, as `resolve` does,
--- to at most sixteen addresses, and try each in the resolver's order,
--- as Go's `Dial` does, until one connects, each attempt within `limit`
--- as `connect_within` limits one, zero for no limit.  When none
--- connects, it fails as the last attempt did.
public connect_by_name: (provider: type is network, host: provider,
                         name: []u8, port: u16, limit: time.duration)
                        -> (connected: io.file)
                        ! unknown_name | refused | unreachable
                        | address_unavailable | timed_out | io.io_failed =
    mut found: [16]address = [16 of unspecified(ipv4, 0)]
    count: usize = try resolve(provider, host, name, port, found[0..<16])
    mut last: attempt_failure = io.io_failed
    for index in usize(0)..<count do
        connected = provider.connect_within(host, found[index], limit)
            else (problem)
            last = problem
            continue
        end
        return
    end for
    fail last
end connect_by_name

--- Connect to the Unix-domain listener at `path`.
public connect_local: (provider: type is network, host: provider,
                       path: text.os_string)
                      -> (connected: io.file)
                      ! refused | invalid_address | unsupported | io.no_access
                      | io.io_failed =
    connected = try provider.connect_local(host, path)
end connect_local

--- Listen for Unix-domain connections at `path`, which the listener makes
--- and the program removes once it is done.
public listen_local: (provider: type is network, host: provider,
                      path: text.os_string)
                     -> (listener: io.file)
                     ! address_in_use | invalid_address | unsupported
                     | io.no_access | io.io_failed =
    listener = try provider.listen_local(host, path)
end listen_local

--- Open a Unix-domain datagram socket bound to `path`.
public open_local_datagram: (provider: type is network, host: provider,
                             path: text.os_string)
                            -> (socket: io.file)
                            ! address_in_use | invalid_address | unsupported
                            | io.no_access | io.io_failed =
    socket = try provider.open_local_datagram(host, path)
end open_local_datagram

--- Send `bytes` as one datagram to the Unix-domain socket at `path`.
public send_to_local: (provider: type is network, host: provider,
                       socket: io.file, bytes: []u8, path: text.os_string)
                      -> none ! refused | invalid_address | timed_out
                              | io.io_failed =
    try provider.send_to_local(host, socket, bytes, path)
end send_to_local

--- Receive the next Unix-domain datagram into `into`, and its sender's
--- path into `room`.
public receive_from_local: (provider: type is network, host: provider,
                            socket: io.file, into: []mut u8, room: []mut u8)
                           -> (count: usize, sender: text.os_string from room)
                           ! timed_out | io.io_failed =
    (count: got, sender: who) := try provider.receive_from_local(host, socket,
                                                                 into, room)
    count = got
    sender = who
end receive_from_local
